## Founding & History
Pipers Brook Vineyard was established in 1973 by regional pioneers Andrew and David Pirie in Pipers River, Tasmania. Other sources state the establishment year as 1974. Dr. Andrew Pirie, Australia's first PhD in Viticulture, founded the winery with his brother David. The Pirie brothers recognized the potential of the Tamar Valley land, noting its terroir's similarity to premium European wine regions like Alsace and Champagne. Pipers Brook was among the first commercial-scale producers in Tasmania and was one of the first close-planted vineyards in the modern Australian industry, initially using an Intermediate Row Spacing (IRS) system with 1.8m rows and 1.25m in-row spacing, resulting in 4,444 vines per hectare.

Kreglinger, a Belgian company, took majority ownership of Pipers Brook in 22001. The de Moor family, based in Launceston, heads Kreglinger and has owned the vineyard since 2001. Andrew Pirie left Pipers Brook in 2003. The winery celebrated its 50th anniversary in 2024.

## People
The current owners of Pipers Brook Winery are the de Moor family of Launceston, who took majority ownership in 2001 through their Belgian-owned company, Kreglinger. Paul de Moor, who moved from Belgium to Launceston in the early 2000s, oversees the business and is the Managing Director of Pipers Brook Vineyard. His family has a long history in wine, with his grandfather, Georges Thienpoint, having bought Vieux Château Certan in Bordeaux in 1924.

Luke Whittle is the current Chief Winemaker, having joined Pipers Brook eight years prior to August 2024, initially for a vintage in 2015 and then appointed Senior Winemaker in 2016, becoming Chief Winemaker in 2019. He oversees the portfolio of wines for Kreglinger Wine Estates. Viticulturist Luciano Caravia joined the team in late 2021, bringing over 16 years of industry experience and a PhD in vine physiology. Kym Ayliffe was appointed viticulturist in 2019 and manages ongoing vine improvement and investment. Sudeep Parial manages the winery under Luke Whittle's direction, responsible for all winery operations from fruit arrival to blending. External consultant Jim Chatto also works with the group.

## Vineyard & Site
Pipers Brook Vineyard has close to 200 hectares of vines across Pipers River and West Tamar. The vineyard sites were chosen for their similarity in soil, aspect, and weather conditions to French regions like Alsace and Champagne. The vineyards benefit from rich volcanic soils, distinct geography, a temperate climate, and proximity to the Bass Strait. The initial plantings in 1974 by Andrew and David Pirie used an Intermediate Row Spacing (IRS) system with rows 1.8m wide and in-row spacing of 1.25m, resulting in 4,444 vines per hectare, making it one of the first close-planted vineyards in modern Australia.

Varietals planted include Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling, and Gewürztraminer. Projections for new plantings are underway, with a focus on increasing Chardonnay and premium Pinot Noir fruit. The farming approach involves continuous improvement and investment into the vines, with a focus on improved sustainability and understanding the relationships between vine, weather, and soil.

## Cellar Door Experience
The Pipers Brook Winery cellar door is open from 10 am to 5 pm, seven days a week, except Christmas Day. Visitors can immerse themselves in Tasmania's cool-climate wine country, with a scenic drive leading to the welcoming cellar door. Guests can explore a portfolio of wines including crisp whites, elegant Pinot Noir, and award-winning sparkling wines. A highlight of the experience is the Wine & Food Pairing Experience.

## Food & Hospitality
Pipers Brook Winery has a café that operates from 10 am to 2 pm, seven days a week, except Christmas Day. The Wine & Food Pairing Experience brings together Tasmanian produce with Pipers Brook's wines, featuring thoughtfully curated pairings with local cheeses, fresh seafood, and seasonal small plates.

## Awards & Recognition
In 2024, Pipers Brook Vineyard's 2017 Late Disgorged sparkling wine was awarded Best Sparkling Wine in Show at the International Cool Climate Wine Show (ICCWS). Andrew Pirie's inaugural 1994 Ninth Island Chardonnay won the white wine trophy at the International Wine Challenge in London. The Pirie sparkling wine label, first made in 1995, became the most awarded of all the wines made by Andrew Pirie and has been served to Queen Elizabeth II on at least two occasions. Andrew Pirie was awarded a Member of the Order of Australia (AM) in 2001 for his services to the Tasmanian wine and tourism industries. He was also a finalist in the QANTAS/Australian Gourmet Traveller Winemaker of the Year Award in both 2002 and 2003. Pipers Brook Vineyard Reserve Chardonnay 2005 was ranked #7 of 15 2005 Chardonnay from Tasmania by The Real Review.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Pipers Brook Vineyard is committed to improved sustainability and deeply cares for its sites, continuously refining winemaking practices. Viticulturist Luciano Caravia is always working towards improved sustainability and understanding the relationships between vine, weather, and soil.

## Visiting Practical Info
The winery's location is 1216 Pipers Brook Rd, Pipers Brook, TAS 7254. The phone number is (03) 6382 7527. The winery is approximately 45 minutes from Launceston. The official website is kreglingerwineestates.com.

## Notable / Distinctive
Pipers Brook Vineyard is considered a modern pioneer of the Tasmanian wine industry. It was the first commercial-scale producer on the island and, at one point, produced about one-third of Tasmania's total wine production. The winery was founded by Dr. Andrew Pirie, Australia's first PhD in Viticulture, who played a significant role in establishing Tasmania's reputation on the world wine scene. The vineyard's terroir is noted for its similarity to premium European wine regions like Alsace and Champagne. Pipers Brook was one of the first close-planted vineyards in modern Australia. The de Moor family, current owners, have a European heritage in wine, with connections to Chateau Vieux Certan in Bordeaux, which is honored in their New Certan Pinot Noir label. The winery has close to 200 hectares of vines, which is considered large by Tasmania's boutique standards.

